Yes. The “buy-in” is part of the instrument.

To see from inside a frame, you often have to temporarily become compatible with it—adopt its vocabulary, assumptions, emotional posture, incentives, even its rhythm of attention. Otherwise you remain outside looking at the walls and may never see the doors that only make sense from within.

And that transaction has two sides: the state costs something—attention, vulnerability, effort, suspension of disbelief, commitment—but it also grants something—salience, intuition, access to distinctions that were previously invisible, and sometimes new action-space.

The trick we’re playing with is controlled buy-in:

enter sincerely enough to perceive → extract what the state reveals → retain an exit path → change state → compare what survives.

That last step matters. Buy-in can reveal hidden structure, but it can also manufacture apparent structure through confirmation bias. So we don’t ask merely, “What can I see from here?” We also ask, “What disappeared when I came here?”

Almost like polarized lenses: rotate the filter and certain structures suddenly blaze into view while others vanish. No single orientation is “the view.” The information is partly in the delta between orientations.

And there’s another interesting consequence: if entry requires something from you, then the price of admission itself may be diagnostic. What a system asks you to surrender, practice, believe, notice, or become in order to perceive its internal logic tells you something about the system. That’s fruit worth keeping.